3 Interactive exercises
Third-party webexercises assets · Course Python integration
Root _template.yml loads assets/webex.css and includes assets/webex.js after the document. These files are derived from webexercises and run in the browser. Outils informatique II additionally uses pywebexercises.exercises to generate exercise HTML from Python. That Python package is a separate dependency, not bundled in the template.
The raw HTML interface below works without Python and describes the contract used by the included JavaScript.

3.1 Text and numeric responses
&lt;label for="crs-answer"&gt;LV95 EPSG code&lt;/label&gt;
&lt;input id="crs-answer" class="webex-solveme" data-answer='["2056"]'&gt;

Try it — enter the EPSG code for LV95 

data-answer must contain a valid JSON array. Matching is exact by default. Add .ignorecase for case-insensitive input or .nospaces to remove ordinary space characters before matching. The current .nospaces implementation removes spaces, not all whitespace.
&lt;input aria-label="Rock type"
       class="webex-solveme ignorecase nospaces"
       data-answer='["basalt", "basalte"]'&gt;
&lt;input aria-label="Approximate result"
       class="webex-solveme" data-answer='[10]' data-tol="0.1"&gt;
Positive data-tol accepts a numeric difference strictly smaller than the tolerance. .regex interprets the joined answer strings as a JavaScript regular expression. Use anchored patterns such as ^basalt$ if a whole response must match. For routine exercises, prefer explicit accepted strings over regular expressions.


3.2 Dropdowns and radio buttons
&lt;label for="unit-choice"&gt;Choose a projected coordinate unit&lt;/label&gt;
&lt;select id="unit-choice" class="webex-select"&gt;
  &lt;option value="blank"&gt;Choose an answer&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;option value="wrong"&gt;Degrees&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;option value="answer"&gt;Metres&lt;/option&gt;
&lt;/select&gt;

&lt;div class="webex-radiogroup" id="unit-radio"&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;&lt;input type="radio" name="unit-radio" value="wrong"&gt;Degrees&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;&lt;input type="radio" name="unit-radio" value="answer"&gt;Metres&lt;/label&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
The correct option must have the literal value answer; dropdown placeholders use blank. Give each radio group a unique, simple ID and use exactly that ID as the name of its inputs. Avoid spaces or punctuation in these IDs because the current script builds a CSS selector from them.


3.3 Check a group and reveal an explanation
&lt;div class="webex-check webex-box"&gt;
  &lt;label for="group-answer"&gt;LV95 EPSG code&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;input id="group-answer" class="webex-solveme" data-answer='["2056"]'&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div class="webex-solution"&gt;
  &lt;button type="button"&gt;Explanation&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;LV95 uses EPSG:2056.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
.webex-check adds a “Show Answers” / “Hide Answers” button and a correct-count indicator. It changes feedback visibility; it does not fill the correct responses into empty controls. .webex-solution requires a button directly inside the container; clicking it toggles the container’s open class.
These controls provide local feedback, not stored assessment results. The answer values are delivered to the browser in the HTML. Native book profiles do not automatically remove webex answer keys. The current JavaScript also resets response boxes when a page loads and assigns window.onload; another script that replaces that handler can stop exercise setup.

5 Fragments and stacked content
Native fragments and r-stack · Local stack-top alignment
.fragment reveals an element when you advance the slide. .fade-out starts visible and disappears. Assigning the same data-fragment-index synchronises changes.
For a replacement at the same position, use this pattern:
## Compare observations

:::: {.r-stack .stack-top}
::: {.fragment .fade-out data-fragment-index="1"}
![](assets/first.png){width=85%}
:::
::: {.fragment data-fragment-index="1"}
![](assets/second.png){width=85%}
:::
::::
At first the first layer is visible. On the next step it fades out as the second appears. Without fade-out, the first image remains behind the second. Use the same mechanism for a callout replaced by a list.
The local .stack-top helper aligns direct layers to the top left, fills their width, removes common paragraph/figure margins, limits images to 70vh, and keeps captions in normal positioning. It also gives fragment-contained callouts and lists full width. It does not create fragment sequencing: r-stack, .fragment, effect classes and indices supply that behaviour.
